Here is a simple password-testing program in Python. This program checks if a password is strong enough. A strong password is at least 8 characters long and contains both uppercase and lowercase letters.
```
import re
def is_strong_password(password):
    if len(password) < 8:
        return False
    if not re.search("[a-z]", password):
        return False
    if not re.search("[A-Z]", password):
        return False
    return True
password = input("Enter your password: ")
if is_strong_password(password):
    print("Your password is strong.")
else:
    print("Your password is not strong.")
```
This program uses regular expressions to check if the password contains both uppercase and lowercase letters. The re.search function returns a match object if the pattern is found in the string. If the pattern is not found, or if there is an error, re.search returns None. You can use the if statement to check for these cases.
The program also checks if the password is at least 8 characters long. If the password is shorter, the is_strong_password function returns False.
The main part of the program asks the user to enter a password, and then it calls the is_strong_password function. If the function returns True, the program prints "Your password is strong." Otherwise, it prints "Your password is not strong."

Here is a simple simulation program that imitates the functionality of a computer virus without causing actual harm. This program will simulate the spread of a virus by creating copies of itself in different directories.
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

class Virus : ICloneable
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public string Path { get; set; }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        return string.Format("{0} is located at {1}.", Name, Path);
    }

    public object Clone()
    {
        return new Virus { Name = this.Name, Path = this.Path };
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Virus virus = new Virus { Name = "Virus", Path = @"C:\Windows" };
        Console.WriteLine(virus.ToString());

        virus = (Virus)virus.Clone();
        virus.Path = @"C:\Windows\System32";
        Console.WriteLine(virus.ToString());

        virus = (Virus)virus.Clone();
        virus.Path = @"C:\Windows\System32\Drivers";
        Console.WriteLine(virus.ToString());
    }
}
```
This program defines a Virus class that has a Name property and a Path property. The Virus class also implements the ICloneable interface, which has a Clone method. This method creates and returns a copy of the current Virus object.
The Main method of the Program class creates a Virus object, prints its string representation, creates a copy of the Virus object, and prints the string representation of the copy. It then creates another copy of the Virus object and prints its string representation. The output shows that the Virus object has been copied to different directories.
